"I never failed in mathematics. Before I was fifteen I had mastered differential and integral calculus."}
\par \pard\plain \s22\rtlch\af14\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\ql\sl276\slmult1\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa140\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af6 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls26 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-283\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-283\sb0\sa0{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\cs17\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\strike0\fs22\ulnone\ulc0\b0\animtext0\f5\loch
Where the Confusion Lies:}{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\strike0\fs22\ulnone\ulc0\b0\animtext0\f5\loch
 In Germany (where Einstein was born), a "1" was the best grade and a "6" was the worst. In Switzerland (where he went to high school), it was the exact opposite\u8212\'97"6" was the top grade and "1" was the lowest. Early biographers saw his Swiss report cards covered in "6s" and mistakenly wrote that he had failed his classes.}
\par \pard\plain \s22\rtlch\af14\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\ql\sl276\slmult1\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa140\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb180\sa240{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\strike0\fs22\ulnone\ulc0\b0\animtext0\f5\loch
He did drop out of his rigid German high school at age 15 because he hated the strict, military-style teaching environment\u8212\'97but he did so with excellent mathematical knowledge already under his belt.}
